when operator gui is connected to gpu port that port must have enabled operator monitor.
  >> DONE - the flag is back ON (screen_3_operator_monitor=true) and the thing that made
     me turn it off is fixed properly (e2ab1a8).
     What locked your mouse: that flag also switches on the pointer-confine helper, which
     read the monitor geometry ONCE at startup and then warped your cursor back into that
     rect every 50ms forever. The layout moved under it (DP-5 was at +3072+0 at startup,
     later at +0+0), so it dragged the pointer into empty space 20x a second - feels like
     a dead mouse, not a fence. Now it re-reads geometry every 2s, rebuilds barriers when
     the monitor moves, and RELEASES if the output disappears.
     BEING STRAIGHT: I caused the lockout. My a35c245 auto-sets operator_monitor from the
     cable, and I did not check that the flag also implies pointer confinement. Also note
     "operator monitor" and "confine pointer to it" are STILL one setting - if you ever
     want the flag without the caged mouse, say so and I will split them.

ndi stream should not have the normal settings rtmp stream has.
srt stream should also have unique options different than rtmp.
  >> DONE per your spec (587ab14), and VERIFIED against your real rig.
     NDI - treated exactly like an SDI out now:
       - Your existing str_2 (type ndi, cabled from PGM 2) is emitted into the generated
         config as <ndi><name>Str2</name></ndi> on the Screen 2 PGM channel. I checked the
         running server's generate after restart - it is there.
       - Always on, no Start/Stop buttons, no encoder fields. The inspector shows only the
         name + a note. Saving an NDI output lights the Apply button, because the consumer
         only exists after regenerate + Apply Caspar config.
       - Cable direction does not matter; disabled/uncabled outputs emit nothing. Two NDI
         senders with the same name are collapsed to one (same-name = on-air conflict).
     SRT - real options, and Start actually works now:
       - FACT FIRST: I checked the binary - bin/casparcg links libsrt-gnutls.so.1.5, so
         Caspar's own ffmpeg opens srt:// directly. The old "Caspar cannot do srt" comment
         in the code was about builds you are not running.
       - New per-output fields (SRT only): latency in MS (default 120, you type ms, the
         server converts to ffmpeg's microseconds - classic footgun, handled in one place),
         streamid, and mode caller/listener.
       - Start for SRT goes through the same encoder pipeline as RTMP with an mpegts
         container. RTMP args are asserted byte-identical in the gate - your on-air
         YouTube path is untouched.
     DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT: an SRT passphrase field. That is a secret, and after the
     YouTube-key-in-logs incident I am not putting another credential in plaintext
     settings. If you need encrypted SRT, say so and I will wire the passphrase through
     the same project-credentials mechanism the RTMP key uses (WO-261).
     TO USE: reload kiosk, then for NDI: Apply Caspar config (the button is lit after you
     save the output). Server already restarted with the new generator.

i still get these fucking stale channels in the generated config
  >> NOW ACTUALLY DEAD (90e58e4) - verified against your live box: the running server
     now generates Screen 1 PGM+PRV, Screen 2 PGM-only, Operator GUI. 4 channels, no
     phantom. You were right three times over; this had THREE separate causes and I owed
     you the last two:
     1. (48ca829) empty-destinations floor - fixed earlier.
     2. Deploy miss BY ME: my second fix (35f6350) landed on disk AFTER the service had
        started, and node caches modules in memory - so every generate you tried today
        ran the OLD code. The file changed; the process never knew. I restarted it.
     3. The real remaining bug, visible once you built your actual rig: the factory
        default seeds the Operator GUI at screen-slot 0, and the Add-destination button
        COUNTED it when picking the next slot - so your first real screen landed at
        slot 1, your second at 2, and slot 0 stayed a permanent hole. Screen count is
        max(slot)+1, so the generator kept filling that hole with a PGM+PRV pair that
        nothing routes to. Fixed both sides: stored configs get their main slots
        re-packed to 0,1,2... automatically (your existing config heals itself - no
        action needed), and the Add button now ignores utility channels when numbering.
     Regenerate + Apply from Device View whenever you like; the config the server hands
     you is already clean. NEEDS KIOSK RELOAD for the Add-button half.

i have a decklink input playing on a look on pgm2 its audio is routed to pgm2 because it
plays there, but muting it or not routing it to pgm2 has no effect on the pgm2 output.
  >> MUTE IS FIXED and live-verified (commit above + service restarted). Full mechanism,
     read off your running Caspar, not guessed:
     - Your look plays the DeckLink input on PGM2 as route://5-4 (host channel 5, layer 4;
       the look layer is ch3-10). The audio arrives EMBEDDED in that video route.
     - The mixer strip's mute sent MIXER 5-4 VOLUME 0 - the HOST channel. A layer route
       taps the source layer BEFORE the host channel's mixer applies volume, so that
       command mutes a mix nobody listens to. Caspar's own log showed your exact clicks.
     - The strip's "routed to PGM2" plumbing is a separate audio-only layer (ch3-324,
       320+slot) which was EMPTY - so unrouting cleared nothing that carried audio.
     Fix: /api/audio/volume now fans the strip's gain out to every live look layer playing
     that exact route. Verified on your box: mute now emits MIXER 5-4 AND MIXER 3-10.
     Retaking a look reasserts the look's own volume - the take stays the authority.
     ONE SEMANTIC LEFT FOR YOU TO DECIDE: the strip's per-PGM route toggles still only
     control the strip's own audio-only routes, NOT audio embedded in a look's video
     route. So "unroute from PGM2" still does not silence a look that plays the input
     with audio - mute does. If you want the route toggle to also gate look-embedded
     audio, that means the take pipeline consulting routing state on every take, and
     retakes would re-silence - say the word and I will spec it properly.
